{% extends "base.jinja" %}
{% block head %}
    <style>
    .nt-fix h3 { margin-top: 2rem; font-size: 1.35rem; scroll-margin-top: 80px; }
    .nt-fix h4 { margin-top: 2.4rem; padding-top: .4rem; scroll-margin-top: 80px; border-top: 1px solid rgba(127, 127, 127, .25); }
    .nt-fix h4:target, .nt-fix h3:target { color: #ffb070; }
    .nt-fix pre { background: rgba(127, 127, 127, .12); padding: .7rem .9rem; border-radius: 6px; font-size: .85rem; white-space: pre-wrap; word-break: break-all; }
    .nt-fix .nt-toc a { margin-right: 1rem; white-space: nowrap; }
    .nt-fix .nt-plain { border-left: 3px solid #4fc3f7; padding: .1rem 0 .1rem .9rem; margin: .8rem 0; }
    .nt-fix ol li, .nt-fix ul li { margin-bottom: .35rem; }
    .nt-fix details { margin: 1rem 0 .5rem; border: 1px solid rgba(127, 127, 127, .3); border-radius: 6px; padding: .5rem .9rem; }
    .nt-fix details summary { cursor: pointer; font-weight: 600; color: #8ab4f8; }
    .nt-fix details[open] summary { margin-bottom: .6rem; }
    .nt-fix .nt-ask { background: rgba(79, 195, 247, .07); border-radius: 6px; padding: .6rem .9rem; margin: .8rem 0; }
    .nt-fix .nt-ask p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
    </style>
{% endblock head %}
{% block body %}
<div class="container nt-fix">
    <h2>How to fix a tracker</h2>
    <p>For people who run a tracker. Each problem below starts in plain words, with things to try that don't need the command line,
        and what to ask your hosting provider if someone else runs the server for you. If you're comfortable with a terminal,
        open <b>For advanced users</b> in each section for exact commands.</p>
    <p class="nt-toc">{% for k, v in titles.items() %}<a href="#{{ k }}">{{ v }}</a> {% endfor %}<a href="#removed">Removed and banned</a></p>

    <h3 id="start">Start here</h3>
    <ol>
        <li><b>Find your tracker's page.</b> In the <a href="/">status table</a>, click the <i class="fa-solid fa-chart-line" style="color:#c15cff"></i>
            icon next to it. The page says what's wrong in plain words, shows exactly what the checks saw, and links to the right section here.</li>
        <li><b>Fix one thing at a time</b>, using the section for your problem.</li>
        <li><b>Press "Check again now"</b> on the tracker's page. It's checked within a minute or two instead of waiting for the next
            regular check (which can be hours away for a tracker that's been down a while). You can do this once an hour.</li>
        <li><b>Give it time.</b> Most statuses clear on the next good check. Up/Bad clears after a few passing tests, and the score
            (Up/Unreliable, Up/Junk) recovers gradually over about a week of steady answers.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Not sure what your tracker runs on? Most public trackers are <b>opentracker</b>, <b>Chihaya</b>, <b>Torrust</b>
        or <b>Aquatic</b> on a rented server (a VPS). If a friend or a company set it up, send them the link to the section below
        that matches your problem: it's written so they can act on it.</p>
    <p class="small">The advanced examples use <code>tracker.example.org</code> and port <code>6969</code>: put in your own.</p>

    <h4 id="no-peers">Hands out no peers (Up/Bad)</h4>
    <div class="nt-plain">
        <p><b>In plain words:</b> your tracker answers, but it doesn't do its actual job: introducing people who share the same torrent
            to each other. Torrent clients that use it don't find anyone through it.</p>
        <p><b>How we know:</b> two test clients in different countries join the same made-up torrent. The second should be told
            about the first. Yours left the first one out in 3 or more of the last 6 tests.</p>
    </div>
    <p><b>Most likely causes, and what to do:</b></p>
    <ul>
        <li><b>It only accepts approved torrents</b> (a "whitelist"), or it's meant to be private and needs a passkey. A public list
            like this one can only use trackers that accept any torrent. If that's deliberate, the tracker simply isn't public:
            nothing to fix, and it'll be removed in time.</li>
        <li><b>It keeps IPv4 and IPv6 users apart</b>, so someone connecting over IPv6 never meets someone on IPv4. Many tracker
            programs have a setting for this.</li>
        <li><b>Something in front of it hides users' real addresses</b>, such as a proxy or load balancer, so every user looks like the
            same address.</li>
        <li><b>It forgets users too quickly</b>, before they announce again.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>If the tracker sends a message with its answers, such as "Require passkey" or "info hash is not authorized", it's shown on its
        page under <b>Message from the tracker</b>, with what it means. Those two mean it's private or only serves its own torrents:
        that's a choice, not a fault, but it can't work as a public tracker.</p>
    <div class="nt-ask"><p><b>Ask your provider or admin:</b> "Our tracker answers but doesn't share peers between clients
        (newtrackon.co.nz reports Up/Bad: no peers). Can you check it accepts any torrent, mixes IPv4 and IPv6 peers in one swarm,
        and sees clients' real IP addresses?"</p></div>
    <details><summary>For advanced users: command line</summary>
    <p>Announce the same torrent twice with different ports. The second reply should list the first peer (<code>6881</code>).
        A <code>failure reason</code> such as "torrent not registered" means it's whitelisted.</p>
<pre>H='%12%34%56%78%9a%bc%de%f0%12%34%56%78%9a%bc%de%f0%12%34%56%78'
U='http://tracker.example.org:6969/announce'
curl -s "$U?info_hash=$H&amp;peer_id=-NT0001-000000000001&amp;port=6881&amp;uploaded=0&amp;downloaded=0&amp;left=0&amp;compact=0"; echo
curl -s "$U?info_hash=$H&amp;peer_id=-NT0001-000000000002&amp;port=6882&amp;uploaded=0&amp;downloaded=0&amp;left=0&amp;compact=0"; echo</pre>
    <p><b>Fix:</b> allow unknown torrents (for opentracker: build without the whitelist option), and hand out peers from the same swarm
        regardless of the address family they announced from. If it's behind a reverse proxy, pass the client address through
        (for example <code>X-Forwarded-For</code>, and configure the tracker to trust it). The status clears after a few passing tests.</p>
    </details>

    <h4 id="fake-peers">Returns fake peers (Up/Bad)</h4>
    <div class="nt-plain">
        <p><b>In plain words:</b> your tracker hands out addresses of "people" who aren't really sharing the torrent. Clients waste
            time trying to connect to them, and downloads look busier than they are.</p>
        <p><b>How we know:</b> the test torrent is made up and only this site knows it, so the only real users are our own test
            clients. Yours returned other addresses for it, 3 or more checks in a row.</p>
    </div>
    <p><b>Most likely causes, and what to do:</b></p>
    <ul>
        <li><b>A "fill" or "padding" option is turned on</b>, which tops up short lists with made-up or old addresses. Turn it off.</li>
        <li><b>Users from different torrents get mixed together.</b> This is usually a bug or a setting in the tracker program; updating
            it often helps.</li>
        <li><b>A cache in front of the tracker</b> (a CDN or caching proxy) replays old answers to everyone. Announces must never be cached.</li>
        <li><b>Users who left are never forgotten.</b> The tracker should drop users who stop checking in.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="nt-ask"><p><b>Ask your provider or admin:</b> "newtrackon.co.nz reports our tracker returns peers for a torrent
        nobody else has. Can you turn off any peer padding or caching of announce replies, and make sure peers expire?"</p></div>
    <details><summary>For advanced users: command line</summary>
    <p>Announce a torrent nobody has (the first command under <a href="#no-peers">Hands out no peers</a>, with a hash you make up).
        The reply should have no peers except, at most, yourself.</p>
    <p><b>Fix:</b> turn off any "fill" or "padding" option, keep peer lists per torrent, expire peers that stop announcing, and don't cache
        announce replies (<code>Cache-Control: no-store</code> on the announce path). The status clears as soon as a check sees only real peers.</p>
    </details>

    <h4 id="dead-address">Dead IPv4 or IPv6 address (Up/Broken)</h4>
    <div class="nt-plain">
        <p><b>In plain words:</b> your tracker's name points to two kinds of address: an older one (IPv4) and a newer one (IPv6).
            One of them works and the other doesn't. People whose computer picks the broken one can't reach your tracker at all.</p>
        <p><b>How we know:</b> we check both addresses separately. One answers; the other has failed several checks in a row.</p>
    </div>
    <p><b>Most likely causes, and what to do:</b></p>
    <ul>
        <li><b>An old or wrong address is still in your domain's settings</b> (the DNS records). In your domain or hosting control panel,
            look at the records for the tracker's name: an <b>A</b> record is the IPv4 address, an <b>AAAA</b> record is IPv6.
            Make sure each one is your server's current address, or delete the one you don't use.</li>
        <li><b>The tracker only listens on one kind of address.</b> This is a setting in the tracker program.</li>
        <li><b>The firewall only lets one kind through.</b> Very common with IPv6: the port is opened for IPv4 but not for IPv6. Many hosting
            control panels have separate firewall rules for each.</li>
        <li><b>The server's IPv6 address changed</b> and the DNS record wasn't updated.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The tracker's page says which one is dead. If you don't need IPv6, deleting the AAAA record is a quick, complete fix.</p>
    <div class="nt-ask"><p><b>Ask your provider or admin:</b> "Our tracker answers on IPv4 but not IPv6 (or the other way round). Can you
        check the DNS records point at the server's current addresses, and that the tracker listens and the firewall is open on both?"</p></div>
    <details><summary>For advanced users: command line</summary>
    <p>List the addresses, then try each family.</p>
<pre>dig +short A tracker.example.org; dig +short AAAA tracker.example.org
curl -4 -s -m 5 -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\n' http://tracker.example.org:6969/announce    # HTTP tracker, IPv4
curl -6 -s -m 5 -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\n' http://tracker.example.org:6969/announce    # HTTP tracker, IPv6</pre>
    <p>For a UDP tracker, this sends the standard UDP tracker "connect" request (<a href="https://www.bittorrent.org/beps/bep_0015.html">BEP 15</a>)
        over IPv4 or IPv6 (the last argument):</p>
<pre>python3 - tracker.example.org 6969 6 &lt;&lt;'EOF'
import os, socket, struct, sys
host, port, fam = sys.argv[1], int(sys.argv[2]), sys.argv[3]
af = socket.AF_INET6 if fam == "6" else socket.AF_INET
addr = socket.getaddrinfo(host, port, af, socket.SOCK_DGRAM)[0][4]
s = socket.socket(af, socket.SOCK_DGRAM); s.settimeout(5)
tid = os.urandom(4)
s.sendto(struct.pack(">QI4s", 0x41727101980, 0, tid), addr)
try:
    print(addr[0], "answered" if s.recv(16)[4:8] == tid else "answered something unexpected")
except OSError as e:
    print(addr[0], "no answer:", e)
EOF</pre>
    <p><b>Fix:</b> make the tracker listen on both families (for example bind <code>::</code> as well as <code>0.0.0.0</code>) and open the
        port for both in the firewall (<code>ip6tables</code>/<code>nft</code> rules are separate from IPv4 ones), or remove the record for
        the family you don't serve. It clears once that address answers.</p>
    </details>

    <h4 id="unreliable">Drops out (Up/Unreliable, Up/Junk)</h4>
    <div class="nt-plain">
        <p><b>In plain words:</b> your tracker works some of the time, but too often it doesn't answer. Users can't rely on it, so it's
            marked down the list. Lots of short dropouts count against it more than one longer outage.</p>
        <p><b>How we know:</b> its score, which counts the last week most, is under 90 (Up/Unreliable) or under 50 (Up/Junk). The
            uptime bars on its page show when it drops out.</p>
    </div>
    <p><b>Most likely causes, and what to do:</b></p>
    <ul>
        <li><b>The tracker program crashes or restarts.</b> Make sure it restarts by itself (most hosting panels and Docker have a
            "restart automatically" option), and look for a pattern: same time every day often means a scheduled job or backup.</li>
        <li><b>It runs on a home internet connection or a cheap server</b> that goes offline, changes address, or runs out of memory.
            A small rented server (VPS) with a fixed address is far more dependable.</li>
        <li><b>It turns away requests when busy</b> (rate limiting set too strictly), so some checks get no answer.</li>
        <li><b>The name sometimes points at an old address</b>: see <a href="#dead-address">Dead IPv4 or IPv6 address</a>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Once it's steady, the score climbs back by itself over about a week. There's nothing to reset.</p>
    <div class="nt-ask"><p><b>Ask your provider or admin:</b> "Our tracker keeps dropping out (newtrackon.co.nz shows Up/Unreliable). Can you
        check the logs for crashes, restarts or running out of memory, make sure it restarts automatically, and that rate limits aren't
        rejecting normal announces?"</p></div>
    <details><summary>For advanced users</summary>
    <p>Run it under a supervisor that restarts it (systemd <code>Restart=always</code>, Docker <code>restart: unless-stopped</code>), check
        <code>journalctl</code> or the container logs and <code>dmesg</code> for OOM kills around the drop times, don't rate-limit announces so
        hard that real requests fail, and give it a fixed address. A UDP tracker needs enough socket buffer for bursts
        (<code>net.core.rmem_max</code>).</p>
    </details>

    <h4 id="slow">Slow (Up/Slow)</h4>
    <div class="nt-plain">
        <p><b>In plain words:</b> your tracker works and is reliable, but answers slowly for most of the world. It's still listed and
            usable; it just isn't among the fastest.</p>
        <p><b>How we know:</b> answers take 300&nbsp;ms or more on average from the four places speed is measured (Oceania, Asia, Europe,
            North America), or enough to pull its score under 90.</p>
    </div>
    <p>This isn't a fault, and often it's simply distance: a tracker in one country is slow from the other side of the world. The latency
        chart on its page shows each region. If it's slow from everywhere, including nearby: the server may be overloaded, or there's a
        slow service in front of it (some protection or proxy services add a lot of delay to HTTP trackers).</p>
    <details><summary>For advanced users</summary>
    <p>Check the server's load and network, avoid chains of proxies in front of an HTTP tracker, and prefer UDP (much less overhead per
        announce). Hosting closer to most users helps most.</p>
    </details>

    <h4 id="down-timeout">Down: timeout</h4>
    <div class="nt-plain">
        <p><b>In plain words:</b> we knocked and nobody answered at all. Either the tracker isn't running, or something is silently
            blocking the way to it.</p>
        <p><b>How we know:</b> no reply within the time limit, from here and from abroad.</p>
    </div>
    <p><b>Things to try:</b></p>
    <ol>
        <li><b>Is the server on?</b> Look in your hosting provider's control panel; restart the server if it's stopped or unresponsive.</li>
        <li><b>Is the tracker program running?</b> If it runs in Docker or as a service, restart it.</li>
        <li><b>Is the port open?</b> The firewall must allow the tracker's port (for example 6969), and for a UDP tracker it must allow
            <b>UDP</b>, not just TCP. This is the most common cause after a server move or a firewall change. Check both the server's own
            firewall and any firewall in the hosting control panel.</li>
        <li><b>Does your provider block it?</b> Some providers block torrent-related ports or traffic. Their terms of service or support
            can tell you.</li>
    </ol>
    <div class="nt-ask"><p><b>Ask your provider or admin:</b> "Our tracker at port 6969 (UDP) doesn't answer from outside. Is the server
        up, is the tracker process running, and is UDP 6969 open in every firewall in front of it?"</p></div>
    <details><summary>For advanced users: command line</summary>
    <p>From a machine outside your network, run the UDP command under <a href="#dead-address">Dead IPv4 or IPv6 address</a>, or the
        <code>curl</code> command for HTTP. On the server, <code>ss -lunp | grep 6969</code> (UDP) or <code>ss -ltnp | grep 6969</code> (TCP)
        shows whether it's listening, and <code>nft list ruleset</code> or <code>iptables -S</code> shows the firewall.</p>
    </details>

    <h4 id="down-refused">Down: connection refused</h4>
    <div class="nt-plain">
        <p><b>In plain words:</b> the server is up, but it said "there's nothing here" on the tracker's port. The tracker program isn't
            running, or it's listening somewhere else.</p>
        <p><b>How we know:</b> the server actively refused the connection.</p>
    </div>
    <p><b>Things to try:</b></p>
    <ol>
        <li><b>Start or restart the tracker program.</b> It may have crashed or not started after a reboot; set it to start automatically.</li>
        <li><b>Check the port.</b> The port in your tracker's address (the number after the colon, such as <code>:6969</code>) must be
            the one the program uses. If you changed it, update the address you've shared, or submit the new one.</li>
        <li><b>Check it listens publicly.</b> A tracker set to listen only on "localhost" or <code>127.0.0.1</code> can't be reached from outside.</li>
    </ol>
    <div class="nt-ask"><p><b>Ask your provider or admin:</b> "Connections to our tracker's port are refused. Is the tracker running, on that
        port, and listening on the public address?"</p></div>
    <details><summary>For advanced users</summary>
    <p>Start it and make it listen on the public address and the port in your announce URL (<code>ss -lunp</code> / <code>ss -ltnp</code>
        shows what's listening where). If you moved it to a new port, you can publish that with a
        <a href="https://www.bittorrent.org/beps/bep_0034.html">BEP 34</a> DNS record.</p>
    </details>

    <h4 id="down-dns">Down: DNS</h4>
    <div class="nt-plain">
        <p><b>In plain words:</b> your tracker's name doesn't lead anywhere any more. It's like a phone number that's been disconnected:
            nobody can find the server, even if it's running fine.</p>
        <p><b>How we know:</b> the name has no address, both here and on public DNS services.</p>
    </div>
    <p><b>Things to try:</b></p>
    <ol>
        <li><b>Has the domain expired?</b> Log in where you registered the domain (your registrar) and check the expiry date. Renew it,
            and turn on auto-renew. This is the most common cause.</li>
        <li><b>Was the record deleted or changed?</b> In the domain's DNS settings, the tracker's name needs an A record (and/or AAAA)
            pointing at your server.</li>
        <li><b>Free dynamic DNS names</b> (like duckdns.org or no-ip) stop working if they aren't renewed or the updater stops. Check your
            account with that service.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>After a fix, DNS changes can take up to a few hours to reach everyone.</p>
    <details><summary>For advanced users: command line</summary>
    <p><code>dig +short A tracker.example.org @1.1.1.1</code> and <code>whois example.org</code> for the expiry date and nameservers.
        <code>dig +trace tracker.example.org</code> shows where resolution breaks.</p>
    </details>

    <h4 id="down-tls">Down: TLS / certificate</h4>
    <div class="nt-plain">
        <p><b>In plain words:</b> your tracker's address starts with <code>https://</code>, and its security certificate (the thing behind
            the padlock in a browser) isn't valid, so clients refuse to connect.</p>
        <p><b>How we know:</b> the secure connection failed.</p>
    </div>
    <p><b>Things to try:</b></p>
    <ol>
        <li><b>Open the tracker's address in a browser</b> (it won't show a page, but the browser will complain about the certificate).
            The warning usually says what's wrong: <b>expired</b>, or <b>for a different name</b>.</li>
        <li><b>Expired:</b> the automatic renewal stopped (very common with free Let's Encrypt certificates). Renew it in your hosting panel
            or ask whoever set it up.</li>
        <li><b>Wrong name:</b> the certificate covers your website's name but not the tracker's (for example <code>example.org</code> but not
            <code>tracker.example.org</code>). Add the tracker's name to the certificate.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>If you'd rather not deal with certificates, a plain <code>http://</code> or <code>udp://</code> address works just as well for a tracker.</p>
    <details><summary>For advanced users: command line</summary>
    <p><code>openssl s_client -connect tracker.example.org:443 -servername tracker.example.org &lt;/dev/null | openssl x509 -noout -dates -subject</code>
        shows the dates and name. A missing intermediate certificate also fails here: serve the full chain (<code>fullchain.pem</code>).</p>
    </details>

    <h4 id="down-http">Down: HTTP error</h4>
    <div class="nt-plain">
        <p><b>In plain words:</b> a web server answered, but with an error message instead of a tracker answer. It's like reaching
            reception, being told "not here", and never getting to the person you called.</p>
        <p><b>How we know:</b> the reply was an error code: 4xx (refused or not found) or 5xx (the server behind it failed).</p>
    </div>
    <p><b>What the error usually means:</b></p>
    <ul>
        <li><b>403 or 503 with a "checking your browser" page:</b> bot protection (for example Cloudflare's challenge) is blocking torrent
            clients, which can't pass a browser check. Turn it off for the tracker's address (<code>/announce</code>).</li>
        <li><b>404:</b> the address is wrong, or the server isn't sending <code>/announce</code> to the tracker program.</li>
        <li><b>502, 503 or 504:</b> the web server is fine but the tracker program behind it is stopped or overloaded: restart it.</li>
        <li><b>429:</b> too many requests: rate limiting is too strict.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="nt-ask"><p><b>Ask your provider or admin:</b> "Our tracker's /announce returns an HTTP error (the code is on the
        newtrackon.co.nz page). Can you check the web server passes /announce to the tracker, that the tracker is running, and that bot
        protection doesn't apply to it?"</p></div>
    <details><summary>For advanced users: command line</summary>
    <p>Run the <code>curl</code> command under <a href="#no-peers">Hands out no peers</a> and read the status and body. Torrent clients can't
        pass a browser challenge, so turn it off for the announce path, and check the reverse proxy's <code>location</code> for
        <code>/announce</code> and its upstream.</p>
    </details>

    <h4 id="down-rejected">Down: the tracker rejects requests</h4>
    <div class="nt-plain">
        <p><b>In plain words:</b> your tracker is running and answers, but every answer is an error message of its own ("invalid
            info_hash", "incorrect request", "torrent not allowed" and so on) instead of a list of peers. To a torrent client it's as
            good as down.</p>
        <p><b>How we know:</b> the tracker's own error text is on its page, under <b>What's wrong</b>. We send standard, valid requests.</p>
    </div>
    <p><b>What the message usually means:</b></p>
    <ul>
        <li><b>"Invalid info_hash", "incorrect request", "bad request":</b> the tracker misreads normal requests. The torrent ID in a
            request is 20 raw bytes, sent URL-encoded, and some tracker programs or the web server in front of them decode it wrongly.
            Usually a bug: update the tracker program, and make sure nothing in front of it (a proxy, a firewall, a "security" filter)
            rewrites or decodes the request.</li>
        <li><b>"Torrent not registered", "not authorized", "unknown torrent":</b> it only serves torrents on its own list. See
            <a href="#no-peers">Hands out no peers</a>.</li>
        <li><b>"Passkey", "login", "not registered user":</b> it's a private tracker. That's fine, but it can't be a public one.</li>
        <li><b>Anything about limits or "too many":</b> rate limiting is set too strictly.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="nt-ask"><p><b>Ask your provider or admin:</b> "Our tracker answers announces with an error (the message is on the
        newtrackon.co.nz page). Can you check the tracker software is up to date and that nothing in front of it alters the request?"</p></div>
    <details><summary>For advanced users</summary>
    <p>Run the <code>curl</code> command under <a href="#no-peers">Hands out no peers</a>: the bencoded <code>failure reason</code> is the
        message. For a UDP tracker, the reply is action 3 with the message after the 8-byte header (<a href="https://www.bittorrent.org/beps/bep_0015.html">BEP 15</a>).
        If <code>info_hash</code> errors only happen for some hashes, look for something decoding <code>%00</code>, <code>%2B</code> or
        non-UTF-8 bytes in the query string (a reverse proxy normalising URLs, or a framework parsing the query as text).</p>
    </details>

    <h4 id="down">Down: no usable answer</h4>
    <div class="nt-plain">
        <p><b>In plain words:</b> something answered, but not like a tracker, or it failed in a way not covered above.</p>
        <p><b>How we know:</b> the exact error is on the tracker's page.</p>
    </div>
    <p>Try the basics: restart the tracker program, make sure the address you've shared (protocol, name, port and the <code>/announce</code>
        part) matches what it actually runs, and that it's a public tracker (one that needs an account or passkey can't be listed).
        If the error on the tracker's page mentions a timeout, refusal, DNS, certificate or HTTP code, see that section above.</p>
    <details><summary>For advanced users</summary>
    <p>The checks above (listening, firewall, the <code>curl</code> or UDP command) usually find it. A UDP tracker that replies to
        <code>connect</code> but not <code>announce</code>, or an HTTP tracker that returns non-bencoded data, shows up here.</p>
    </details>

    <h3 id="removed">Removed and banned</h3>
    <p>A tracker is removed from the list when it hasn't answered for <b>5 days</b>, or when it answers so rarely that it's no use
        (under 15% once dropouts are counted). It's then <b>banned for 30 days</b>: submitting it again is refused until then. Its
        page still says when and why it was removed, and when it can come back. A tracker turns dark grey in the
        <a href="/">status table</a> once it's 2 days from removal, so there's still time to fix it. See the <a href="/submitted#banned">banned trackers</a>.</p>
    <p>Once the ban ends, fix the problem and <a href="/">submit the tracker again</a>: it's checked like any new tracker.
        Questions: see the <a href="/faq">FAQ</a>.</p>
</div>
